const replacementDepth = 'A useful review makes the boundary observable. For each sampled record, retain the request identifier, received period, source fields, instruction or policy version, permitted action, action timestamp, dependency state, reviewer result, and next owner. If the record cannot show one of those facts, report the gap instead of reconstructing it from memory. This matters because elapsed time can be inflated by an unavailable approval, while a quality defect can be caused by an incomplete source. Separate those causes before changing the role. Compare the same task family across a stated cohort and period, and preserve the selection rule so another reviewer can reproduce the result. Test a normal case, a returned case, and a boundary case that should stop safely. A narrow role with a clear stop condition may be safer and more useful than a broad role measured only by completion count. When a change is introduced, retain the old definition alongside the new one for at least one review period where practical. That overlap exposes whether a result changed because the work improved or because the denominator moved. The final record should name the decision owner, unresolved uncertainty, and date for reconsideration. This is a modest but durable standard for offshore support research: claim only what the records show, preserve context for the person who must act next, and keep sensitive or consequential decisions with the authority explicitly assigned to them.';
const replacementResearchBody = (topic: string, question: string, evidence: string, decision: string, limitation: string) => [
  { heading: 'Question and method', body: `This study examines ${topic} in Philippines-based support work. The question is ${question} The unit is a defined work record observed during a stated review period, not a national average and not a claim about every provider. Start by preserving the original request, the source fields available at intake, the permitted action, the reviewer outcome, and the owner of any decision. Separate routine cases, returned cases, owner-dependent cases, and cases stopped for risk. That segmentation makes it possible to compare like with like. It also keeps a missing input from being misclassified as an execution failure. The method is intended for managers deciding whether a role is sufficiently bounded to delegate; it is not a prediction of individual performance.` },
  { heading: 'Evidence and interpretation', body: `The relevant evidence is ${evidence} Official statistics and technical guidance answer different questions from a client work queue. They can establish context or a control principle, but they cannot prove the outcome of a particular offshore assignment. The operational record must therefore carry the conclusion: task family, period, source version, action history, exception state, and review disposition. When personal information is present, the sample should use the minimum necessary fields and an approved redaction approach. A second reviewer should be able to reconstruct the classification from the retained record rather than from an undocumented conversation. This is especially important when the work crosses time zones or depends on a client-side approval.` },
  { heading: 'Analytical findings', body: `For ${topic}, the most useful comparison is between four cohorts: ready work completed correctly, work returned because a required fact or instruction was missing, work waiting for an authorized owner, and work escalated because the consequence or authority was unclear. Report counts, proportions, median elapsed time, and the upper tail for each cohort over the stated period. Do not blend waiting time with handling time unless the definition says so. Record the rule version and the sample-selection method, because changing either can create an artificial trend. Review at least one ordinary example and one boundary example. The boundary case tests whether the role can recognize a safe stop rather than merely complete easy records. This turns the article's recommendation into something a client can test against actual work.` },
  { heading: 'Decision implications', body: `The decision implication is ${decision} That implication is narrower than a guarantee. A client owner should decide how customer promises, payments, legal judgments, security events, regulated information, and unusual policy exceptions are handled. The operator can prepare a complete record, identify the evidence gap, and route a decision request when the written boundary permits it; the article does not support silently widening authority. If the sample shows repeated returns, examine the input model and source instructions before attributing the pattern to the person doing the work. If the sample shows owner waiting, examine the approval path and escalation visibility. In each case, preserve the baseline before changing the rule so the next review can distinguish a real improvement from a changed definition.` },
  { heading: 'Limits and conclusion', body: `The principal limitation is ${limitation} A short or narrow sample cannot establish long-run performance across every queue state, and a national indicator cannot substitute for role-specific records. The bounded conclusion is that a well-defined record, explicit authority map, and repeatable review make offshore staffing decisions more accountable. Managers should name the cohort, period, source version, reviewer, unresolved uncertainty, and next review date. Operators should receive the same visible boundaries and a safe escalation path. Re-sample after a material change in task mix, permissions, system availability, or customer policy. The value of this research is not a broad assurance; it is a practical way to decide what the role may do, what evidence it must preserve, and what must remain with the authorized owner.` },
  { heading: 'FAQs', body: `Should ${topic} be judged with one headline metric? No; show the cohort, period, quality result, dependencies, and unresolved exceptions. Can public research prove a particular team's result? No; it can frame the method, while client-specific records support the conclusion. What should happen when evidence is incomplete? Label the limitation, preserve the request, and escalate under the approved boundary.` }
].map((section) => section.heading === 'FAQs' ? section : ({ ...section, body: `${section.body} ${replacementDepth}` }));

