// New, family-scoped August 17 Research batch. Each record directly owns the campaign date.
const august17ResearchBatchBase: readonly ResearchPost[] = [
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-call-quality-calibration', title: 'Call Quality Calibration for Philippines-Based Support', excerpt: 'A research question about whether reviewers score the same support call the same way, and what that means for a distributed quality program.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Scope Benchmarks', stats: ['Agreement should be measured by case type, not one blended score', 'A calibration sample cannot prove every future call will be scored consistently'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'Do client and Philippines-based reviewers apply the same quality rule to comparable support calls? The useful unit is a redacted call or transcript paired with the rubric version, case type, reviewer decision, and reason for any disagreement. A high average score can hide a rule that different reviewers interpret differently, especially when the queue mixes routine requests with policy exceptions.' },
    { heading: 'Evidence scope and method', body: 'Use a stated review period and stratify the sample by channel, case type, outcome, and escalation status. Have two reviewers score a common subset independently, then record agreement by rubric dimension. Compare accuracy, required identity checks, explanation of the next step, case notes, and escalation separately. The public evidence base includes NIST guidance on attributable access and the ILO discussion of homeworking, but those sources provide control context, not a prediction of call performance.' },
    { heading: 'What the comparison can show', body: 'Disagreement is useful when the reason is recorded. It may show an unclear definition, missing context in the recording, a policy that has changed, or an execution problem. Reviewers should not resolve a disagreement by silently changing the score. Preserve the original decisions, agree the rule with the client owner, and mark the effective date of the revision. Keep customer promises, refunds, legal interpretations, and security events outside the calibration role unless the authority is explicit.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'A small common sample estimates reviewer consistency for the selected cases. It cannot establish that every reviewer, queue, language, or future policy will behave the same way. Recording quality, transcription errors, and case mix can affect the result. National labor or connectivity statistics cannot be used as proxies for an individual agent or reviewer.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'A quality program is easier to trust when it measures both the work and the measurement process. Use a common sample, version the rubric, publish disagreement reasons, and re-test after a material rule change. The decision is whether the role and review boundary are clear enough to delegate, not whether a single percentage proves service quality.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Is reviewer agreement the same as accuracy? No. Agreement can be high around a wrong rule. Should every call receive two reviews? Not necessarily; use a common calibration sample and adjust review depth for risk and defect history.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-shift-coverage-variance', title: 'Shift Coverage Variance in Philippines Support Operations', excerpt: 'How to separate roster coverage, queue demand, and avoidable delay when evaluating a Philippines-based support schedule.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Scope Benchmarks', stats: ['Coverage is a time-window claim, not a headcount claim', 'Average hours do not establish service coverage for a specific queue'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'When a support queue misses its intended coverage window, is the cause staffing availability, demand timing, access failure, or an owner dependency? Treat coverage as the overlap between an agreed operating window and the time a permitted task could actually be handled. Do not infer it from scheduled hours alone.' },
    { heading: 'Evidence scope and method', body: 'Collect the roster window, queue arrival time, first eligible action, blocked intervals, handoff time, and closure state for a defined period. Separate routine cases from escalations and compare like days. PSA labor statistics and World Bank digital-access research offer Philippine labor and infrastructure context, but neither source establishes an individual shift result. The client queue records must carry the conclusion.' },
    { heading: 'Interpretation', body: 'A late response may reflect no scheduled coverage, a missing permission, a waiting approval, or a connectivity interruption. Report those states separately. If the role is expected to prepare work but not decide policy, owner waiting should not be counted as operator handling time. A coverage plan should also specify what happens when a case arrives outside the window and who owns the next safe action.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'A short period may overrepresent holidays, launches, outages, or unusually quiet demand. Timestamps can also reflect system events rather than human action. Do not turn a historical coverage profile into a promise without checking current queue mix and the client’s operating rules.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'The useful decision is whether the planned Philippines-based role covers the actual demand window with a visible fallback and a named owner for blocked work. Preserve the denominator, the time zone, and the reason for every gap before changing the schedule.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Does a full shift guarantee coverage? No. Access, demand timing, dependencies, and handoffs still matter. Should national average hours set a schedule? No; use the client’s queue records and explicit coverage window.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-rework-cause-analysis', title: 'Rework Cause Analysis for Philippines-Based Operations Support', excerpt: 'A bounded method for telling incomplete inputs, unclear instructions, source errors, and execution mistakes apart.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Workflow Design', stats: ['Returned work needs a reason code before it becomes a performance signal', 'Rework rates change when the task definition or denominator changes'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'What explains returned work in a Philippines-based operations queue, and which causes can the role change? The question is narrower than whether the operator is “good.” A return may begin with a missing source field, an ambiguous instruction, a permission mismatch, a system defect, or a step performed incorrectly.' },
    { heading: 'Method', body: 'Sample accepted and returned records from a stated period. Preserve the original input, instruction version, permitted action, reviewer reason, and customer or downstream impact. Code the first material cause, then record contributing causes separately. Use a second reviewer for disputed categories. NIST access guidance is relevant when deciding whether the operator had the right permission, while PSA and World Bank materials remain context rather than proof of individual performance.' },
    { heading: 'Decision use', body: 'Match the remedy to the cause. Add a required input when information was absent, revise the instruction when the rule was unclear, repair a source mapping when the data was wrong, and change access only through the authorized owner. Coach execution only when the record shows the inputs and rule were available. A safe stop on an undefined exception is evidence of control, not rework.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'Reviewer labels are judgments and can drift when examples are missing. A narrow sample may miss rare but serious errors. Changes to the task or taxonomy break a simple time series, so report the change rather than presenting a false trend.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'Rework analysis helps a manager decide whether to improve the brief, the source, the access map, or the review path. It should not become a shortcut for blaming offshore staff for failures the record cannot assign to them.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Can one record have several causes? Yes. Name a primary cause and contributing factors. Is a lower return rate always better? Not if reviewers stopped recording defects or the work became easier.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-customer-language-escalation', title: 'Customer Language Escalation in Philippines Support Teams', excerpt: 'What a distributed support role can document about language fit without turning accent or nationality into a quality judgment.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Hiring Controls', stats: ['Language escalation should use observable comprehension evidence', 'A customer preference is not evidence of an agent defect'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'Which support interactions need a language or communication accommodation, and how can the team route them fairly? The measure should focus on whether the customer understood the permitted next step, not on accent, nationality, or a vague impression of “fit.”' },
    { heading: 'Evidence scope and method', body: 'Review a defined sample of calls, chats, or emails with the customer’s stated difficulty, the channel offered, the explanation given, the case outcome, and the escalation reason. Use redacted records where possible. The ILO provides context on work arrangements, and accessibility or privacy duties may apply to the client’s market, but public sources cannot determine a particular interaction’s quality.' },
    { heading: 'Analysis', body: 'Separate a product or channel barrier from an agent communication error. A customer may need written confirmation, slower turn-taking, interpretation, or a manager decision. The Philippines-based role can record the request and use approved alternatives. It should not diagnose a customer, promise an unsupported accommodation, or treat a preference as proof that the agent failed.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'Self-reported difficulty is important but does not explain every cause. A small sample may overrepresent escalated cases. Transcripts also lose tone and timing, so use the original channel evidence when the distinction matters.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'A fair language review protects customers and workers when it records the barrier, the available channel, the comprehension outcome, and the owner of the next decision. It gives the client a better basis for training or channel changes than a broad label about a person.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Should accent be scored? Not as a standalone quality measure. Should a language request bypass identity controls? No. Keep the approved identity path and escalate only the communication barrier.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-access-removal-timing', title: 'Access Removal Timing for Philippines-Based Support Roles', excerpt: 'A research model for measuring whether role changes and exits lead to timely, attributable access removal.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Hiring Controls', stats: ['Removal timing needs a defined trigger and system list', 'A completed ticket is not proof that every active session ended'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'How long does it take to remove the systems a Philippines-based support role no longer needs, and where do gaps remain? The trigger may be an exit, transfer, inactivity threshold, or permission change. The study should define the trigger before measuring elapsed time.' },
    { heading: 'Method', body: 'For a stated period, compare the trigger record with identity, CRM, ticketing, telephony, file, remote-access, token, and recovery-method logs. Record the owner, timestamp, exception, and evidence of verification. NIST identity and least-privilege guidance supports this control model. It does not establish a universal time limit for every organization.' },
    { heading: 'Findings to separate', body: 'Distinguish notification delay, owner delay, system delay, failed revocation, and verification gap. A person removed from one queue may still retain a session or group membership elsewhere. Keep the role narrow, use named accounts, and route exceptions to the client security owner. Do not copy credentials or sensitive records into a research log.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'Logs differ by system and time zone. Some providers retain administrative evidence outside the client’s direct view. A review can show the records available to it, not prove that an unseen integration has no access.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'The practical result is a system-by-system removal record with a trigger, owner, timestamp, verification step, and open exception. A role is not fully offboarded because one account was disabled.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Is disabling the email account enough? No. Check every approved system, session, token, group, and authenticator. Should the operator own their own removal? No. The authorized owner should control and verify it.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-work-sample-validity', title: 'Work-Sample Validity for Philippines Staffing Decisions', excerpt: 'How to tell whether a work sample tests the real role instead of rewarding familiarity with a toy exercise.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Scope Benchmarks', stats: ['A sample supports only the task and conditions it represents', 'Validity depends on a clear rubric and comparable inputs'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'Does a proposed work sample represent the decisions, evidence, tools, and boundaries of the Philippines-based role? A polished exercise can still be weak if it omits the source ambiguity, handoff, approval limit, or customer consequence that makes the real work difficult.' },
    { heading: 'Evidence scope and method', body: 'Map the sample to representative tasks, required inputs, permitted actions, expected evidence, and exception rules. Use the same rubric for comparable candidates and redact personal information. Record the instruction version, time allowed, reviewer, result, and unanswered questions. Public labor statistics may describe context, but they cannot validate an individual’s result.' },
    { heading: 'Interpretation', body: 'A valid sample tests the role’s real work without granting production access or asking for unpaid business output. It should include at least one ordinary case and one boundary case that should be escalated. Score reasoning only where the rubric defines it, and avoid protected-trait or nationality shortcuts. Keep final hiring judgment with the authorized decision-maker.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'One sample cannot predict every future case. Candidate familiarity with the format, language, technology, or time limit can affect results. The exercise should therefore be one part of a documented process, not a claim of certainty.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'A work sample is useful when it mirrors the actual role closely enough to test judgment while remaining safe and fair. The strongest result names what was tested and what remained outside the evidence.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Should a sample use live customer data? No. Use synthetic or redacted inputs. Is speed the main measure? Only when the real role has a defined time requirement and quality remains visible.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-owner-wait-time-analysis', title: 'Owner-Wait Time in Philippines-Based Support Work', excerpt: 'A research question about when queue aging reflects an approval dependency rather than slow execution.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Workflow Design', stats: ['Elapsed time must separate handling from dependency waiting', 'An escalation timestamp needs a named response owner'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'How much of a support item’s elapsed time belongs to permitted work, and how much belongs to waiting for a client owner? Combining those periods creates a misleading performance signal and can pressure an operator to make a decision outside the role.' },
    { heading: 'Method', body: 'For a defined queue and period, capture receipt, first action, escalation, owner response, resumed work, and closure. Classify the item as routine, returned, blocked, or owner-dependent. The record should include the reason for waiting and the authority required. NIST access principles support preserving attributable actions, while official Philippine labor data does not answer a client-specific queue question.' },
    { heading: 'Analysis', body: 'Report handling time and dependency time separately. Review the age distribution, not only the average, and identify whether the same approval type recurs. If waiting dominates, the change may belong in the approval path or service definition. If permitted handling is slow, examine input quality, system access, and task complexity before attributing cause to the operator.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'Timestamp quality varies across tools, and people may update records late. A historical sample also cannot prove that a new escalation rule will reduce future waiting until it is tested.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'A visible waiting state is a management control. It preserves fairness for the Philippines-based operator and tells the client where a decision or policy definition is holding the queue.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Should waiting time be removed from all reporting? No. Report it, but label it separately. Can an operator close a case while waiting? Only if the approved workflow defines a safe holding state.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-source-version-control', title: 'Source Version Control for Philippines Operations Support', excerpt: 'How to test whether a distributed operator uses the current approved source when policies and records change.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Workflow Design', stats: ['A citation needs a version or effective date to be reproducible', 'An old answer can be accurate historically and wrong operationally'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'Can a Philippines-based operator identify the current approved source for a recurring task, and can a reviewer tell which version informed the action? This matters when policy, product, customer, or supplier records change while older copies remain searchable.' },
    { heading: 'Method', body: 'Select a defined question cohort and period. Record the source identifier, effective date, retrieval time, answer given, and reviewer result. Test ordinary questions, superseded sources, conflicting instructions, and missing owners. NIST guidance on attributable access supports the record trail; it does not replace the client’s source-governance rule.' },
    { heading: 'Analysis', body: 'Separate retrieval failure from source-governance failure. If the current source is unavailable, the safe result is a stop and escalation. If an old source remains marked current, the owner must repair the library. Do not ask support staff to resolve contradictory policy by personal judgment, and do not make a customer commitment from an unverified copy.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'A controlled question set may not represent customer language or rare policy exceptions. Source status can also change after the review window, so publish the evidence date and recheck after material changes.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'Version control turns a knowledge claim into an inspectable record. The decision is whether the role has a current source, a safe fallback, and an owner who can resolve ambiguity.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Is a link enough? No. Keep the source identity and effective date. Should the operator choose between conflicting policies? No. Route the conflict to the source owner.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-backup-channel-safety', title: 'Backup Channel Safety for Philippines Remote Support', excerpt: 'What continuity evidence is needed before a backup communication path can be used during a remote-work interruption.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Hiring Controls', stats: ['A fallback channel must preserve identity and evidence rules', 'Connectivity resilience does not justify moving sensitive data to personal tools'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'Can a Philippines-based support role continue safely when the primary system or connection is unavailable? The answer depends on what may be done offline, what must stop, how identity is verified, and where the record is restored. A second channel by itself is not continuity.' },
    { heading: 'Evidence scope and method', body: 'Run a tabletop exercise with a representative task, a defined outage condition, an approved fallback, and a named recovery owner. Record what information moved, what action was paused, how the timestamp was preserved, and how the final record was reconciled. World Bank digital-economy research gives historical connectivity context, while NIST guidance informs authentication and access controls.' },
    { heading: 'Interpretation', body: 'The safest fallback may be a documented pause rather than a broader permission. If a backup channel cannot preserve customer identity, privacy, or audit evidence, use it only for an outage notice and route the work later. Do not transfer passwords, MFA codes, full customer exports, or unapproved screenshots into personal messaging or storage.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'A tabletop does not prove the system will behave the same way during a live outage. Connectivity and vendor conditions can change. Repeat the test after a material system, location, or role change.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'Continuity is credible when the fallback has a narrow purpose, a safe stop, a recovery record, and an owner. It is not credible when the only plan is to improvise in a less controlled channel.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Should a backup channel bypass MFA? No. Can offline work include customer data? Only if the approved design explicitly permits it and protects the data. Who decides when to resume? The named owner.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
  { slug: 'philippines-offshore-approval-boundary-testing', title: 'Approval Boundary Testing for Philippines Support Roles', excerpt: 'A research framework for finding whether a distributed role can prepare work without quietly becoming the decision-maker.', published: '2026-08-17', thumbnail: '/research/research-default.svg', cluster: 'Hiring Controls', stats: ['A role boundary needs a test case, not only a sentence in a brief', 'Safe escalation is part of successful task completion'], sections: [
    { heading: 'Research question', body: 'Can a Philippines-based support role complete permitted preparation and stop at the point where client authority is required? A role may appear narrow on paper but still invite hidden judgment when examples, approval thresholds, or exception owners are missing.' },
    { heading: 'Method', body: 'Create a small set of synthetic or redacted cases: one routine case, one incomplete-input case, one conflicting-source case, and one consequential exception. Record the permitted action, evidence attached, stop point, escalation wording, and reviewer disposition. NIST least-privilege guidance supports the access boundary; the client’s own policy defines the decision authority.' },
    { heading: 'Findings', body: 'A good result is not always a completed transaction. It may be a complete packet, a clear question for the owner, or a documented stop. Score whether the operator recognized the boundary and preserved enough context for the next decision. Do not reward a guess merely because it makes the queue look finished.' },
    { heading: 'Limits', body: 'Synthetic cases cannot capture every customer or commercial consequence. A test also shows behavior under stated instructions, not every future situation. Re-test when tools, permissions, or approval rules change.' },
    { heading: 'Conclusion', body: 'Boundary testing gives managers evidence about whether a role is ready to delegate. It also gives the operator a fairer definition of success: complete the permitted work, preserve evidence, and escalate what belongs elsewhere.' },
    { heading: 'FAQs', body: 'Is escalation a failure? No. When authority is missing, a well-documented escalation is the controlled result. Should every exception be solved in the brief? No. Recurring exceptions need rules; rare ones need a named owner.' }
  ], sources: commonSources },
];
